so this is a super interesting story  that is getting a lot of attention in  the last 24 hours I was even on rising  this morning talking about this with  crystal ball and cigar and jetty and the  story is that House Speaker Nancy Pelosi  of course a Democrat said yesterday that  she would be comfortable with Bernie  Sanders as the Democratic presidential  nominee if indeed he ends up being the  selection of the Democratic primary  voters in what's left of this 2020  Democratic primary and she was asked  this just as she was walking out of a  meeting of the House of Representatives  and she simply said yes answering the  question yes she would be comfortable  and this is interesting for a few  different reasons and before we go on  with the discussion also note that  Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer was  asked a similar question and he sort of  seemed to be saying the same thing he  said quote look the bottom line is very  simple we have a lot of strong nominees  and I'm not supporting one over the  other but I think every one of them will  be will beat President Trump or would be  better than President Trump so not a  ringing endorsement but certainly both  Schumer and Pelosi the Democratic  leaders in the respective House Senate  and House are saying they would at least  be okay with a Bernie Sanders nomination  so there's a few different angles here  to talk about first of all is okay I  mean they recognize that Bernie of all  the candidates has the clearest path to  the nomination it's certainly clearer  than any other individual candidate  right now and so they're just getting in  line this is a relatively cynical  interpretation but I think one that is  not a irrationally cynical they want to  be on the inside not the outside if  Bernie Sanders ends up as the nominee  and in particular if Bernie Sanders ends  up being president so in one sense you  could say we don't really have to pay  much attention to Pelosi and Schumer  saying this because it is a cynical  strategic decision of the centrist  establishment Democrats to make sure  that they don't end up getting the door  shut on them if Bernie Sanders becomes  nominee or president and that's  obviously part of it there is no  question that they don't want to be on  the outside if Bernie's the nominee but  there's a very important secondary  in here if you look at the 538 odds  right now there are roughly equal odds  of Bernie securing the nomination before  the DNC with 1991 delegates or more and  roughly equal odds of there being nobody  who gets to 50% plus one if there is  nobody who gets to 50% plus one  delegates before the DNC the Democratic  establishment is going to need to decide  whether they want to contest the  convention and try to deny Bernie  Sanders the nomination either through  the Alliance of the delegates of various  centrist candidates or with the addition  of the super-delegates on the second  round of voting who could just hand the  nomination to somebody else so the  importance of Nancy Pelosi and Schumer  signaling we're at least okay with a  Bernie nomination maybe a sign that the  Democratic establishment is not going to  try to contest or broker the convention  in order to deny Bernie the nomination  even if he has a plurality of the  delegates that in and of itself is a  crucial decision because that could have  a huge effect on the general election it  is conceivable that if the Democratic  Party is seen as heavy-handed and going  out of their way to deny the nomination  to the person with the most delegates if  that person is indeed Bernie Sanders  there is a risk that that is going to  reduce turnout in November and help  Donald Trump win it is a risk that it  will turn some Democratic voters who  support Bernie Sanders in the primary  away from voting at all in November or  even towards writing somebody in third  party to voting for Donald Trump even  and when you start to see party leaders  saying things like Nancy Pelosi saying  I'd be comfortable with Bernie as the  nominee this could be a very important  sign that they recognize that it could  be a self-destructive action to take the  nomination from Bernie Sanders or to be  seen as doing that even if it's not in  practice what you believe is going on  now at the same time could it this could  be something that is being said by  Pelosi and Schumer to make us think they  won't contest the convention when in  fact they plan to but that's sort of  like to layer  of cynicism maybe it's too much cynicism  there's also a potential lesson here  from Republicans in 2016 because when  you think back to the Republican primary  in 2016  remember that for a whole bunch of that  primary the full Republican  establishment including Fox News and a  lot of right-wing media they were united  against Donald Trump instead of with  Donald Trump  now they ultimately came around to him  because anything was better than Hillary  in their eyes and Donald Trump ended out  just barely squeaking out an electoral  college victory because of about 77,000  votes in three key states it's not crazy  to think that if the DNC were to simply  rally behind whoever is the nominee and  even behind whoever is looking like they  have the best shot at being the nominee  which right now is Bernie they might be  saying even if we don't like Bernie even  if we're not actually comfortable with  Bernie as the nominee if Republicans  were able to get Trump elected even  after being against them almost all the  primary and coming to him at the end may  be our best shot at removing Trump is  just getting behind whoever is the  nominee regardless of whether they are  from what they perceive to be a  different wing of the left or the  Democratic Party in the end it's  important that Nancy Pelosi is even  willing to say this at this point in  time even if it's completely cynical  even if it's completely self-serving and  out of her motivation not to end up on  the outside of the Democratic Party if  indeed Bernie Sanders becomes the  nominee or president it is still  important that she's saying this at this  point in time if you have a different  interpretation of what Pelosi and  Schumer signaling at least acquiescing  to the idea of a Bernie Sanders  candidacy let me know I do want to hear  from you
 

